// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not allow inline comments at the end of open statements → Comments must be on a separate line above the statement.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not permit global coverage facts unless required by requirements; enabling assumptions must be placed only in scenario predicates if not universal.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 scenario predicates can encode existential coverage assumptions (e.g., "some r: Request | ...") for scenario-specific requirements.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 assertions and scenario predicates can co-exist to separate universal and scenario-only requirements.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not allow the 'in' operator with set comprehensions like User & (u: User | u.isAdmin = True); must use {u: User | u.isAdmin = True} for set comprehension.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 requires all predicate parameters to have explicit type annotations and to be separated by commas; cannot use parameters without types in predicate headers.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not support parameterized open of util/seq (e.g., open util/seq[Request]) → Must use open util/seq as Seq and use Seq/Seq[Type] for sequence-typed fields.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not allow inline comments at the end of open statements → Comments must be on a separate line above the statement.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 requires all predicate parameters to have explicit type annotations and to be separated by commas; cannot use parameters without types in predicate headers.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not support parameterized open of util/seq (e.g., open util/seq[Request]) → Must use open util/seq as Seq and use Seq/Seq[Type] for sequence-typed fields.
// [LESSON]: After open util/seq[Request], type Seq[Request] is directly in scope—do not prefix with util/seq/ or alias.
// [LESSON]: When defining functions in Alloy 6 with set comprehensions as bodies, always use parentheses and terminate with a semicolon to avoid top-level syntax errors.
// [LESSON]: When a function must return a single value (e.g., one Clearance), its body must be a deterministic single-expression (not a set), and Alloy 6's max[] operator can be used to select the unique highest value among a set.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6: Function definitions with curly brace bodies must be terminated with a semicolon to prevent parser errors at subsequent top-level declarations.
// [LESSON]: Alloy 6 does not have a built-in max[] operator; must use explicit "if in S" checks to find the highest value in a set.
// [LESSON]: In Alloy 6, never use but blocks in run or check commands; encode all scenario-specific constraints inside predicates or facts.

